<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parex Bank</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1992–2010</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parex Bank was a Latvian commercial bank founded in Riga in 1992 that grew into the country's second-largest bank, with many deposits from non-resident customers. During the financial crisis of 2008 it suffered a run and was taken over by the state in November 2008, and the cost of its rescue helped force Latvia to seek an emergency loan from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. Its viable assets were later transferred to Citadele Bank.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parex-bank.jpg" alt="Parex Bank" width="205" height="130" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Parex Banka · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parex_Banka.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
